concepts.body:
A **unit test** is a small piece of code that runs one part of your program, such as one method, and checks the result is what you expect. If it isn't, the test fails and tells you. With JUnit, a test is a method marked `@Test`. That `@` marker is an **annotation**: a label that tools like JUnit look for. `assertEquals(expected, actual)` fails the test when the two values differ:

```java
class PriceTest {
    @Test
    void addsTax() {
        assertEquals(120, Price.withTax(100));
    }
}
```

The Surefire plugin runs unit tests in the `test` phase. It picks up classes in `src/test/java` whose names end in `Test` or `Tests` (or start with `Test`). For JUnit 5, add `org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter` with `test` scope; Surefire 3 finds it by itself. Write Surefire's version in your POM, as in the example, because the version Maven picks on its own can be old.

Choosing what runs:

- `mvn test -Dtest=PriceTest` runs one class; `-Dtest='Price*Test#adds*'` picks classes and methods by name, with `*` matching anything.
- `-DskipTests` skips tests; `-Dmaven.test.failure.ignore=true` keeps building even when tests fail.
- Surefire's `<configuration>` block controls which classes run, settings handed to the tests, and running several tests at once.

Reports go to `target/surefire-reports/`, as readable text plus XML files other tools can read. What tests print is saved there too, so look there when a test fails.

An **integration test** checks several parts working together, such as your code plus a real database, so it is slower. The **Failsafe** plugin runs these: classes named `*IT` (for Integration Test), in the `integration-test` phase. You must add Failsafe to your POM yourself, as in the example, and run `mvn verify` to include it.

Failsafe doesn't stop the build the moment a test fails. It waits until `verify`, so the phase in between (`post-integration-test`) can still shut down anything the tests started, such as a test database.

concepts.code:
// src/main/java/com/acme/GreetMojo.java in a project with <packaging>maven-plugin</packaging>
package com.acme;
import org.apache.maven.plugin.*;
import org.apache.maven.plugins.annotations.*;
import org.apache.maven.project.MavenProject;
import java.nio.file.*;
@Mojo(name = "greet", defaultPhase = LifecyclePhase.GENERATE_RESOURCES, threadSafe = true)
public class GreetMojo extends AbstractMojo {
    @Parameter(property = "greet.name", defaultValue = "World")
    private String name;
    @Parameter(defaultValue = "${project.build.directory}/greeting.txt", required = true)
    private Path output;
    @Parameter(defaultValue = "${project}", readonly = true)
    private MavenProject project;
    @Override
    public void execute() throws MojoExecutionException {
        try {
            Files.createDirectories(output.getParent());
            Files.writeString(output, "Hello, " + name + " from " + project.getArtifactId());
            getLog().info("Wrote " + output);
        } catch (java.io.IOException e) {
            throw new MojoExecutionException("could not write greeting", e);
        }
    }
}
// consumer: <plugin><groupId>com.acme</groupId><artifactId>greet-maven-plugin</artifactId>
//   <version>1.0.0</version><executions><execution><goals><goal>greet</goal></goals></execution></executions></plugin>
// $ mvn generate-resources -Dgreet.name=Ada   -> [INFO] Wrote .../target/greeting.txt
